    "profile": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: Light Energy Into Sugar\n\nINTRO: Photosynthesis is the reaction that builds sugar from air and water using light. Here is the reaction, its energy source, and where it happens — then a quick practice check.\n\nSECTION 1 - The reaction: raw materials to sugar\nRule: photosynthesis builds glucose, a sugar, from two raw materials — carbon dioxide and water. Glucose is the plant's stored food; carbon dioxide comes in from the air and water comes up from the soil. Both are matter, not energy, so neither one on its own makes the reaction go.\nEXAMPLE: Arrow chain: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen. The plant keeps the glucose and lets the oxygen go. Try it: Name the two raw materials photosynthesis starts with. Answer: carbon dioxide and water.\n\nSECTION 2 - Light energy and chlorophyll\nRule: light energy, usually from the sun, drives the reaction. Chlorophyll, the green pigment inside chloroplasts in leaf cells, absorbs that light. Chlorophyll is not the fuel: it captures the light and passes the energy into the reaction. With no light there is no energy input, so no glucose.\nEXAMPLE: Analogy for the layering: a video game controller is the sensor (chlorophyll), the console's power supply is the energy (light), and the score on screen is the output (glucose). The controller does not power the console by itself. Try it: Where is chlorophyll found? Answer: inside the chloroplasts, mainly in leaf cells.\n\nSECTION 3 - Leaves and the oxygen by-product\nRule: photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves, the plant's largest light-catchers. Oxygen is released as a by-product: it is leftover from the reaction, not the goal. It leaves the plant through the stomata, the pores in the leaf surface.\nEXAMPLE: Basketball analogy: the legs supply the energy (light), the hands direct it (chlorophyll), the ball through the hoop is the result (glucose), and the swish is the free extra (oxygen). Try it: A leaf has green patches and white patches with no chlorophyll. Under light, which patches release oxygen? Answer: only the green patches, because only they can absorb the light energy.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Check this summary equation by counting atoms: 6CO2 + 6H2O -> C6H12O6 + 6O2.\n  1. Carbon: the left side has 6 molecules of CO2, each with 1 carbon, so 6 x 1 = 6 C. The right side has 6 C inside one glucose. Balanced.\n  2. Hydrogen: the left side has 6 molecules of H2O, each with 2 hydrogens, so 6 x 2 = 12 H. The right side has 12 H inside glucose. Balanced.\n  3. Oxygen: the left side has 6 x 2 = 12 O from CO2 plus 6 O from water, giving 12 + 6 = 18 O. The right side has 6 O in glucose plus 6 x 2 = 12 O in the six O2 molecules, giving 6 + 12 = 18 O. Balanced.\n  ANSWER: 6 C, 12 H and 18 O on each side. Light energy is added, and glucose plus oxygen come out; no atoms are gained or lost.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: It has water and carbon dioxide, so a plant in a dark cupboard still makes glucose and gives off oxygen. Why: the raw materials are matter, not energy; the reaction still needs light energy for chlorophyll to absorb, and in the dark that input is zero. Right: in the dark, water and carbon dioxide sit unused — no light energy, so no glucose is built and no oxygen is released.\n\nSUMMARY: Photosynthesis: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen, driven by light energy that chlorophyll in leaf chloroplasts absorbs; oxygen is the by-product.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which part of the leaf actually absorbs the light energy for photosynthesis? [choices: Chlorophyll inside the chloroplasts | Glucose stored in the leaf | Oxygen leaving through the stomata | Water arriving from the roots] -> Chlorophyll inside the chloroplasts\n2. A healthy plant sits in a sealed clear box with plenty of carbon dioxide and water, but the box stays in a dark cupboard for a week. Which outputs of photosynthesis stop, and what is missing? [typed answer] -> Both glucose production and oxygen release stop, because there is no light energy for the chlorophyll to absorb.",
    "mirror": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: How Plants Make Their Own Food\n\nINTRO: Photosynthesis is the way plants make glucose, a sugar they use for food. This lesson shows what goes into that process, where it happens, and what comes out.\n\nSECTION 1 - The recipe: carbon dioxide plus water\nThe rule: photosynthesis makes glucose, a sugar the plant uses for food, from carbon dioxide and water. Carbon dioxide is a gas in the air, and water is taken in by the roots. The plant uses light energy from the sun to run this reaction. The word equation is carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. Oxygen is a gas released as a by-product, an extra thing made along with the glucose.\nEXAMPLE: Video game crafting recipe: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. The left side lists what you put in; the right side lists what you get.\n\nSECTION 2 - Where it happens: leaves, chloroplasts, chlorophyll\nPhotosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves. Inside leaf cells are tiny green parts called chloroplasts. Chloroplasts hold chlorophyll, the green substance that absorbs light energy. Absorb means to take in. Chlorophyll catches the light so the leaf can use that energy to make glucose.\nEXAMPLE: Basketball picture: a pass must be caught before a player can score. Chlorophyll is like the catcher: it catches light energy so the leaf can make its food. Partly worked: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + ____. The last product is left for you. Answer: oxygen.\n\nSECTION 3 - The leaf factory and the oxygen by-product\nLeaves are the main place where photosynthesis happens because they are flat and catch light. The plant takes carbon dioxide in through tiny holes in the leaf. Water travels up from the roots to the leaf. Glucose is used by the plant for energy or stored, and oxygen leaves the leaf into the air. Oxygen is called a by-product because it is not the main food the plant is making.\nEXAMPLE: Drawing comics: the leaf is the main panel where the action happens. Oxygen is a small extra panel that pops out at the side. Try it: What gas is released from the leaf as a by-product? Answer: oxygen.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A plant has water and carbon dioxide, and sunlight hits its leaves. Write the word equation for photosynthesis and name the gas the plant releases.\n  1. Start with the two materials the plant takes in: carbon dioxide and water. Reason: these are the starting materials named in the rule.\n  2. Add the energy source: light energy from the sun. Reason: the plant needs light to drive the reaction, and chlorophyll absorbs it.\n  3. Write the two products: glucose and oxygen. Reason: glucose is the sugar food the plant makes, and oxygen is the by-product.\n  4. Put them in the word equation: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. Reason: starting materials go on the left, products on the right.\n  5. Check the gas released: oxygen is on the product side, so oxygen is released. Reason: the by-product leaves the leaf.\n  ANSWER: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen; the plant releases oxygen.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants get their food from the soil. Why: soil supplies water and minerals, but it does not supply glucose; glucose is made in the leaves from carbon dioxide and water using light energy. Right: photosynthesis makes glucose in the leaves from carbon dioxide and water plus light energy.\nWrong: Photosynthesis takes in oxygen and gives out carbon dioxide. Why: the two gases are swapped; oxygen is the by-product that leaves the plant, and carbon dioxide is a starting material taken in. Right: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen.\nWrong: Chlorophyll is the tiny part of the leaf cell that holds water. Why: chlorophyll is the green substance that absorbs light; the tiny parts that hold it are chloroplasts, and water is a starting material, not what chlorophyll holds. Right: chlorophyll inside chloroplasts absorbs light energy.\n\nSUMMARY: Photosynthesis makes glucose from carbon dioxide and water using light energy. Chlorophyll in chloroplasts absorbs the light, and oxygen is released as a by-product. It happens mainly in leaves.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which gas do plants take in from the air to make glucose? Hint: it is the gas people breathe out. [choices: Carbon dioxide | Oxygen | Nitrogen | Water vapor] -> Carbon dioxide\n2. What is the green substance inside chloroplasts that absorbs light energy? Hint: it is the substance, not the tiny part that holds it. [typed answer] -> chlorophyll\n3. A learner writes: 'Photosynthesis takes in oxygen and gives out carbon dioxide.' What is wrong with that statement? Hint: match each gas to whether the plant takes it in or releases it. [choices: Nothing is wrong; plants take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide. | The gases are swapped: carbon dioxide is taken in, and oxygen is released. | Plants do not take in any gas during photosynthesis. | Oxygen is changed into glucose inside the leaf.] -> The gases are swapped: carbon dioxide is taken in, and oxygen is released."
